 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
  <h3 id="questions-title">Quiz: radar, warning and countermeasures</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next question</button></div>
 </section>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Why does a medium-PRF mode see more blind speeds than a high-PRF one?</summary><p>Its spectral lines are closer together, so its clutter filters at those harmonics cancel more closures. It has fewer range ambiguities in return.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> A group is called 6,000 ft below you at 30 NM. Where must the volume reach?</summary><p>At least 2° down: 6,000 = 30 × elevation × 100.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Why might a crew stay in track-while-scan rather than lock?</summary><p>It keeps tracks on several targets while still scanning, and an active missile can be guided from it; a lock gives continuous data on one target but stops the search.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> You have a solid lock on a contact. Do you know what it is?</summary><p>No. A lock gives continuous track data, not an identity. An IFF reply can identify a friend; anything else needs other sources.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> During a hard defensive turn two symbols for the same radar appear. Why?</summary><p>Aggressive maneuvering changes the signal strength at each antenna quickly, and the processor can read one emitter as several, each kept for its age-out time.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> Why dispense chaff during the turn to the beam rather than flying straight at the radar?</summary><p>A Doppler radar ignores chaff, which stops in the air at once, unless you too have no relative velocity, which happens only on the beam.</p></details>

